insert into public.articles (slug, title, excerpt, body_md, tags, min_level, published_at)
values
  ('after-you-lost-your-temper', 'After You Lost Your Temper', 'You shouted, the room went quiet, and now you feel sick about it. What is happening in your body afterward, how to repair it properly, and how to look at the pattern underneath.', 'You raised your voice. Maybe you slammed a door, or said the sharp thing you had been holding for weeks. The room is quiet now and your hands are still shaking, and you feel sick.

There are two separate jobs in front of you. One is the repair with the other person. The other is working out what happened, so that the next time is further away and smaller. They are easier if you do them in that order, and not in the first ten minutes.

What the shame is doing

The hot wave that arrives after an outburst is one of the most uncomfortable feelings there is, and it pushes people toward two unhelpful moves.

The first is to minimize. It was not that bad, they provoked me, everyone shouts sometimes. That protects you from the feeling and leaves the other person holding it alone.

The second is to collapse into it. I am a terrible person, I am just like my father, I ruin everything. That feels like accountability and it is not, because it turns the conversation into one about reassuring you.

What you want instead is something narrow and plain: I lost my temper, it was not okay, and here is what I am doing about it.

Let your body come down first

For a while after an outburst you are not capable of a good conversation, and this is physical rather than a matter of willpower.

Adrenaline takes a surprisingly long time to clear. Your heart is still fast, your attention is still narrow, and the part of you that can hear someone else''s side is not fully back online yet. Apologies made in that state tend to turn into round two.

So do something with the thirty minutes. Walk, wash up, sit on the back step, move something heavy. Not scrolling, which keeps you in your head, and not rehearsing the argument, which tops the adrenaline up.

You will know you are back when you can say the other person''s point out loud without your chest tightening.

What a real repair sounds like

Short, specific, no conditions.

Name what you did: I shouted at you in the kitchen. Name the effect without guessing: that must have been frightening, or I know that made you shut down. Say what you will do differently: I am going to leave the room when I feel it building, and come back when I can talk.

Then stop. Do not add the part about what they did. If there is something you need to raise about their behavior, it is a separate conversation on a different day, and bolting it onto an apology cancels the apology.

If a child was involved, the repair matters more rather than less. Children do not need parents who never lose their temper; they need to see what it looks like when an adult takes responsibility for it.

Look at the hour before, not the moment

People usually examine the explosion. The useful information is almost always in what came before it.

Were you hungry, short of sleep, in pain, two days into a bad week? Had you been carrying something unsaid for two weeks? Were you already over capacity before the small thing happened?

Anger that seems disproportionate to the trigger is almost always proportionate to something else, and the something else is usually either exhaustion or a boundary that has been quietly crossed for a long time.

Write down the last three times it happened and look for the overlap. Most people find one or two conditions that are present nearly every time, and those are workable in a way that "be less angry" is not.

Have an exit you have already decided on

In the moment, you will not invent a good strategy. You will use the one you have practiced, so pick it now, while you are calm.

The most reliable one is leaving. Not storming out, which reads as punishment, but saying a short agreed line and going: I am getting too heated, I need ten minutes, I am coming back. Then actually come back, because the returning is what makes it trustworthy rather than abandoning.

Tell the people you live with what the line is and what it means, so it is a plan you share rather than a move you spring on them.

And lower the stakes of the conversations themselves. Hard topics go better earlier in the day, after food, sitting down, one subject at a time.

When it is more than a bad moment

Some of this is beyond self-help, and it is worth being honest with yourself about which.

If anyone is frightened of you, if you have broken things or made contact with someone in anger, if you are drinking when you are angry, or if this is a pattern you have tried and failed to change on your own, please talk to a doctor or a therapist, and say plainly what has been happening. Anger responds well to work; the specific skills are teachable, and the people who teach them are not shocked.

That is also true if the anger sits on top of something older. Growing up around rage, or around someone you had to manage, leaves a nervous system that goes from calm to furious with very little in between, and that is treatable rather than a character flaw. A counselor is the right place to take it, and going does not mean you are the monster you felt like this afternoon.', array['Anger', 'Shame']::text[], 0, '2026-10-10T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz),
  ('the-time-of-year-it-happened', 'The Time of Year It Happened', 'Some dates carry weight. Why the weeks around an anniversary can knock you sideways before you have even noticed the calendar, and how to plan for one rather than be ambushed by it.', 'You are irritable, sleeping badly, and oddly tired, and you cannot think why. Then you see the date on something and it lands: it is nearly a year, or five, or twenty, since the accident, the diagnosis, the day they died, the night it happened.

This is one of the most common experiences there is and one of the least talked about, so people tend to assume they are going backward. You are not. Your body is keeping a kind of calendar, and it often knows before you do.

Why the calendar matters

Memory is not only words. It is light, temperature, smells, the sound of the heating coming on, what is on the trees.

So when the season comes round, a great deal of what was present at the time is present again, and your nervous system responds to all of it at once. That is why an anniversary reaction can feel physical and arrive without a thought attached: a heaviness in the chest, broken sleep, jumpiness, a short fuse, a flatness you cannot argue with.

Holidays and family dates do the same work. The first birthday, the first Christmas, the day you always went somewhere together.

It often starts before the date

The hard part is frequently the run-up rather than the day itself, which catches people out.

Two or three weeks beforehand, the dreading starts, usually unrecognized. People find themselves snapping, withdrawing, drinking a bit more, working later, unable to settle to anything. Then the actual day arrives and is sometimes quieter than expected, and the week after can be the flattest of all.

Knowing that shape in advance takes a surprising amount of the frightening out of it. Nothing has gone wrong; this is what the body does with a date.

Decide what the day is for

The worst version of an anniversary is the one nobody has planned, where you pretend it is an ordinary Tuesday and then fall apart at four in the afternoon.

So make a decision, even a small one, about what the day holds. Some people want ritual: the grave, the beach, the song, cooking what they cooked. Some want distraction and company. Some want to be alone with the photographs, and some want to be at work with their hands busy.

All of those are legitimate. What helps is that you chose it, rather than being carried by the day.

Build in the exits too. If you are going somewhere with people, decide in advance how you will leave if you need to, and tell one person so you do not have to explain in the moment.

Plan the whole week, not just the date

Treat the surrounding days as part of it.

Protect sleep where you can, because an anniversary week with three bad nights in it is a different proposition. Keep the week''s demands low: this is not the week to host, interview, move, or have the difficult conversation you have been putting off.

Put two or three ordinary, steadying things in it on purpose. A walk at the same time each day, a swim, a meal with someone easy. Not a project, just structure, because structure is what holds a week like this up.

And expect to be worse at everything for a few days. Slower, more forgetful, less patient. Plan around it rather than being disappointed by it.

Tell one or two people

The silence around anniversaries is the part that does the extra damage, because the people closest to you often have no idea what week it is.

So say it plainly: next Thursday is the anniversary, I am not great in the run-up, I may be quiet. That is usually all it takes, and it turns an unexplained bad mood into something the people around you can be kind about.

If you are the friend rather than the person, the message to send is the simple one. Thinking of you this week. No question in it, nothing required back.

What helps in the moments it comes at you

When it surges, the aim is not to argue it away but to be where you are: in a room, in October, years later.

Get your feet flat on the floor, breathe out longer than you breathe in, and name five things you can see. Cold water on your wrists or face is oddly effective. So is moving: a walk around the block will do more than an hour of trying to think your way out.

And if the memory itself is coming at you in pictures, remind yourself in plain words that it is a memory, it is in the past, and you are here now. Say it out loud if you can.

When to get help with it

Anniversary reactions usually ease off within a few weeks. If yours does not, if each year is getting heavier rather than lighter, or if the memories are intruding during the day, the sleep is not coming back, or you are keeping yourself going on alcohol, please take it to a doctor or a therapist.

Grief and trauma both respond well to treatment, including years afterward, and there is nothing unusual about asking for help for something that happened a long time ago. Say to whoever you see that this time of year is when it is worst; it is information they will know exactly what to do with.

And if the week has taken you somewhere dark, and you are not sure you want to be here, please tell a doctor or someone you trust today rather than waiting. In the US you can call or text 988 at any time.', array['Trauma', 'Grief']::text[], 0, '2026-10-10T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz),
  ('when-washing-and-dressing-feels-like-too-much', 'When Washing and Dressing Feels Like Too Much', 'The shower, clean clothes, brushing your teeth: the ordinary things go first when your mood is low. Why that happens, and how to lower the bar until they are possible again.', 'It is the middle of the afternoon and you have not washed. Not because you decided not to, but because the distance between where you are sitting and the bathroom is somehow enormous, and has been for several days.

Then the shame starts, which does not make the shower any closer. This is one of the most common parts of a low mood and one of the least discussed, because nobody wants to say it out loud.

Why the ordinary things go first

Low mood is not only sadness. It takes your energy, your ability to start things, and the small amount of reward you normally get from doing them.

Washing and dressing are quietly complicated: several steps, a decision or two, a change of temperature, and often standing up for longer than you can face. When starting anything is hard, a seven-step task is harder than it looks, even though you have done it ten thousand times.

And none of it feels worth it, because the low mood has flattened the payoff. Normally clean clothes feel good; right now they feel like nothing, so your brain cannot see the point.

That is the mechanism, and it is worth knowing, because it means this is a symptom rather than a verdict on your character.

Lower the bar on purpose

The trap is all-or-nothing: a proper shower, hair washed, fresh everything, or nothing at all. Nothing at all is what usually wins.

So make the bar deliberately small and keep it there until it is easy.

A sink wash with a cloth counts. Dry shampoo counts. Wipes count. A shower where you do not wash your hair counts, and sitting down in it counts too, which is what a plastic stool in the bath is for and far more people use than will admit it.

Changing your top but not the rest of it counts. Changing into different pajamas counts as changing.

These are not failures at the real thing. They are the real thing, scaled to what you have today.

Make the steps nearly automatic

Take the deciding out, because deciding is the expensive part.

Put one set of clothes out the night before, or keep a small pile of the easiest ones at the front: no buttons, no choices, nothing that needs ironing. Wear the same thing three days running if that is what makes getting dressed possible.

Keep a bath towel, a cloth and the basics within reach of where you actually sit, not in the cupboard you have to go through two rooms to find.

And attach it to something that already happens: after the kettle goes on, after the dog, after the first time you go to the bathroom anyway. A thing linked to another thing requires much less from you than a thing from a standing start.

Teeth are the one worth protecting

If you can only hold on to one of these, hold on to your teeth, because this is the one that becomes expensive and painful rather than just uncomfortable.

Keep a toothbrush somewhere other than the bathroom if that is what it takes: by the bed, by the sink in the kitchen. Brush for twenty seconds instead of two minutes. Brushing without toothpaste is better than not brushing. Chewing gum when you genuinely cannot is better than nothing.

Nobody who understands low mood thinks less of a person for any of this.

Use the part of the day you have most of

Most people have a window where the day is slightly easier, often late morning or early evening. Spend it on this rather than on something that can wait.

And expect no reward, at first, from doing it. The feeling of being pleased about being clean may not show up for a while, which is the illness and not a sign the effort was pointless. Do it on the basis that it reliably makes the next hour slightly less heavy, which is usually true even when you cannot feel it.

Then notice afterward, out loud if you can: I washed, and that was hard, and I did it.

What to say to yourself about it

The sentence most people reach for is some version of a grown adult should be able to shower, and it does not get anyone into the bathroom.

Something plainer works better: this is the illness taking the easy things first, so I am going to do the small version, and that will do for today. Flat and factual, not a pep talk.

If you have been here before, remind yourself that the ordinary things came back last time, usually not because you forced them but because the mood lifted a little and they got easier.

When to get help

Please take it to a doctor if the low mood has been sitting on you for more than a couple of weeks, if sleep, appetite or the basics have all gone, or if the days have stopped containing anything you would call living.

This is one of the clearest signs that something is worth treating, and it is also one of the things people most often leave out when they finally do see someone. Say it plainly: I have not been able to wash or get dressed for a while. A doctor or a therapist will take it as useful information rather than as something embarrassing, and low mood responds well to treatment.

And if things have become dark enough that you are not sure you want to be here, please tell a doctor or someone you trust today. In the US you can call or text 988 at any time.', array['Depression', 'Self-Care']::text[], 0, '2026-10-10T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz),
  ('researching-a-decision-instead-of-making-it', 'Researching a Decision Instead of Making It', 'Six weeks of reviews, spreadsheets and forum threads, and still no decision. How to tell useful research from avoidance, and how to set the stopping rule before you start.', 'You have read everything. The comparison tables, the forum threads, the long review from the person who clearly knows what they are talking about. You have a spreadsheet.

And you have not decided, and it has been six weeks, and the problem you were solving is still there.

Research is the most respectable form of putting something off, because it looks exactly like diligence. The difference is worth learning to spot in yourself.

What the research is actually doing

Up to a point, information makes a decision easier. Past that point it does something else: it postpones the moment where you commit and could turn out to be wrong.

That is the real fear in most stuck decisions. Not picking badly, but being the person who picked badly, having had every chance to get it right.

So the reading becomes a way of staying in the comfortable place where the decision is still open and nothing can be your fault yet. It even feels productive, which is why it can go on for months.

The signs you have passed the useful point

A few are reliable.

You are rereading. The same review, the same thread, the same two options going round, and you are no longer learning anything new.

The sources have started to contradict each other and you are looking for the one that agrees with what you already want.

You have moved from the thing to the meta-thing: not which bike, but how to decide which bike. Not which job, but which framework to use on which job.

And the deadline keeps drifting quietly: another week, after the weekend, once this is out of the way.

If two or three of those are true, more reading is not what is missing.

Decide what would change your mind

This is the most useful question in a stalled decision: what piece of information would actually change my answer?

Often there is one, and it is specific and findable. Whether it fits in the space. What it costs to leave. Whether the hours are negotiable. Go and get that one thing and stop reading everything else.

And often, when you ask the question honestly, there is nothing. You already know what the options are and the differences between them are small or unknowable. That is not a research problem; it is a decision that needs making, and no amount of further reading will make it feel safe.

Set the stopping rule before you start

Decide the bounds up front, because they are impossible to set once you are deep in it.

An hour of reading, three options, and then I pick. A week to look, decide on Sunday. Whatever I know by Friday is what I will decide on.

Writing it down somewhere you will see it is worth more than you would think. So is telling one person, because a stopping point somebody else knows about is much harder to slide past.

Then at the end, pick with what you have. Not the perfect answer; the best answer available at the time you agreed to stop.

Sort the reversible from the rest

A lot of this effort goes into decisions that could simply be undone, and that is where the real waste is.

Things you can return, cancel, resign from, repaint, move again: decide those fast and badly on purpose. The cost of a wrong choice is a small amount of hassle, and it is almost always less than another three weeks of your attention.

Save the careful, slow version for the genuinely hard-to-reverse ones: the mortgage, the surgery, the move across the country, the business you will sign for. Those deserve the spreadsheet. Very little else does.

And notice which kind you are treating this as. Most people apply the big method to the small decisions and then have nothing left for the big ones.

Decide how you will live with it afterward

Half of the dread is about the aftermath rather than the choice, so settle that part in advance.

Agree with yourself that you will not keep rechecking once it is done: no going back to the reviews, no pricing the other option, no reading the thread again. Those feed the idea that the decision is still open when it is not.

And agree what you will do if it turns out to be wrong. Usually the honest answer is small: I would sell it, I would change again in a year, I would live with it and be mildly annoyed. Saying that out loud shrinks the whole thing, because the catastrophe you were protecting against turns out to be an inconvenience.

When it is bigger than this one decision

If this is how most choices go, if the stuckness has cost you money, opportunities or a relationship, or if the not-deciding is keeping you awake, it is worth looking at with someone rather than trying harder on your own.

Perfectionism, a fear of being judged, and a background of being criticized for getting things wrong all produce exactly this, and all of them are workable. A therapist is the right place to take it, and it is a common enough thing to bring that you will not have to explain why it matters.', array['Perfectionism', 'Decision Making']::text[], 0, '2026-10-10T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz),
  ('hating-photographs-of-yourself', 'Hating Photographs of Yourself', 'A photo comes up and your stomach drops. Why a picture lands so much harder than a mirror, what the checking afterward does, and how to stay in the pictures anyway.', 'Someone sends the photos from the weekend. You scroll to the one with you in it and your stomach drops, and then you spend ten minutes on it: the angle, the arms, the face you were apparently making.

By the end you are not thinking about the weekend at all. You are thinking about whether that is what everyone sees.

Why a photo lands harder than a mirror

A mirror is familiar and a photograph is not, and most of the shock is in that gap.

In a mirror you see yourself reversed, moving, from your own height, with your face arranged. A photo is a single frozen hundredth of a second, usually from someone else''s angle, often from below, in whatever light was there, with a lens that flattens or stretches depending on how close it was.

It is not more accurate than the mirror. It is a different distortion, and an unfamiliar one, so it reads as the truth you had been hiding from yourself.

Add that you are the only person in the photo you examine. Everyone else gets a glance.

What you are comparing it to

Almost nobody compares a photo of themselves to other real photos. They compare it to pictures that have been chosen, posed, lit and smoothed, including the ones their friends post.

You are also comparing it to a version of yourself that updates slowly. Most people carry a mental picture that is a few years old, sometimes decades, and a current photograph argues with it. That stings in a way that has very little to do with how you actually look.

And mood changes the whole thing. The same photo on a flat, tired day looks worse than it does on a good one, which is a sign you are seeing your mood rather than your face.

The checking is what makes it stick

What people do after a bad photo is the part that does the damage.

Zooming in. Comparing it with an old picture. Looking in the mirror to check. Turning sideways. Asking someone whether it is accurate, then not believing the answer. Deleting every copy and then finding it again.

All of that narrows your attention down to the part you dislike, which makes it bigger and more distressing every time you look. Checking never produces reassurance that lasts, and the relief it gives is what keeps you doing it.

The useful move is to put the phone down and leave the room. Not forever, just now, before the twenty minutes happens.

What to do in the moment

Name what is happening plainly: I am having a bad reaction to a photograph. That sentence is small and it puts a little distance in.

Then widen your attention on purpose. Look at the whole picture rather than the one part: where you were, who was there, what you were laughing about. The photo is a record of a day, and you have been reading it as a verdict on your body.

Do something with your hands and your body for a few minutes, because this is a feeling that fades much faster when you move than when you sit with your phone.

And leave the deciding about the photo for later. Whether it gets posted, kept or deleted is a question for tomorrow, not for the ten minutes when your stomach is in your shoes.

Be in the pictures anyway

The avoidance is understandable and it has a real cost.

People who dislike photographs of themselves tend to stop being in them, which means years go by with no record of them with the people they love. Then they look back and the absence is the thing that hurts, not the arms or the chin.

Children in particular do not look at a photo of a parent and assess their body. They look for the person who was there.

So be in them, badly, often. The more ordinary pictures of yourself you have, the less power any single one has, partly because familiarity is what takes the shock out.

Change what you say out loud

Many people narrate their own appearance constantly in company, as a joke or a pre-emptive apology, and it does two things.

It rehearses the belief, so you hear your own worst line about yourself several times a day. And it teaches the people around you, including children, that this is how a person talks about their own body.

You do not have to swap it for compliments you do not believe. Dropping the commentary is enough. If something has to go in its place, make it factual and about the day: that was a good afternoon, I like that we are all in it.

When it is bigger than a photograph

If the photos are keeping you out of your own life, if you cannot be in a picture at all, or if you are spending a long time every day checking, comparing, or planning what to do about your body, that is worth taking to someone.

Please talk to a doctor or a therapist if how you feel about your body is driving what you eat, whether you leave the house, or whether you see people, or if it has been getting louder rather than quieter. Those feelings respond well to treatment, and the people who work with them hear this every day and will not find it vain or silly.

And if it has become dark enough that you are not sure you want to be here, please tell a doctor or someone you trust today rather than carrying it alone.', array['Self-Compassion', 'Body']::text[], 0, '2026-10-10T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz)
on conflict (slug) do update set
  title        = excluded.title,
  excerpt      = excluded.excerpt,
  body_md      = excluded.body_md,
  tags         = excluded.tags,
  min_level    = 0,
  published_at = excluded.published_at;
